+# Bug Assessment: Category deletion removes products and breaks references
+
+- **Slug**: ticket
+- **Created**: 2026-11-18T00:00:00+00:00
+- **Source**: pasted text
+- **Verdict**: valid
+- **Severity**: critical
+
+## Report (verbatim or summarized)
+
+A merchant deleted an old “Summer sale” category. Products that belonged only to it disappeared, carts containing them stopped working, and orders containing them could not be opened. The deleted category returns a server error instead of 404. DELETE `/api/v1/private/category/{id}` must remove only the category and descendants, retain products and their data/other categories, preserve carts and orders, and return 404 for missing categories and subsequent category/product listings.
+
+## Symptom
+
+`CategoryServiceImpl.delete` deletes products when they have no remaining category, and deletes a category without reliably detaching products from all deleted categories. This violates product retention and can leave cart/order product references invalid. Missing-category deletion currently resolves through `getOne` and is expected to produce 404, while deleted-category reads must remain 404 rather than fail through stale associations.
+
+## Reproduction
+
+1. Create a category with a child category and products, including a product assigned only to the parent/child and a product assigned to a surviving category as well.
+2. Delete the parent through `DELETE /api/v1/private/category/{id}`.
+3. Verify the products, stock/prices, carts, and orders remain usable; verify deleted categories and their product listings return 404/empty as specified. Delete an unknown id and verify 404.
+
+## Suspected Code Paths
+
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/services/catalog/category/CategoryServiceImpl.java:231-286` — category deletion collects descendants, then calls `productService.delete` for products with only deleted-category memberships; this is the direct data-loss cause.
+- `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/store/facade/category/CategoryFacadeImpl.java:360-368` — DELETE resolves the category before invoking the service and supplies the not-found boundary.
+- `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/store/facade/category/CategoryFacadeImpl.java:114-145` and `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/repositories/catalog/product/ProductRepositoryImpl.java` — category-based reads depend on category/product associations and must see detached products/categories after deletion.
+- `sm-core-model/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/model/catalog/product/Product.java:110-136` — product/category is a many-to-many association without product deletion semantics; it should be updated, not the product entity removed.
+- `sm-core-model/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/model/catalog/category/Category.java:63-67` — descendants are cascaded from the category hierarchy, so deleting the category tree is separate from product retention.
+
+## Root Cause Hypothesis
+
+Confidence: high. The deletion loop explicitly calls `productService.delete(dbProduct)` when the product has one or fewer categories, treating category membership as product ownership. It also removes associations one category at a time and relies on a possibly stale product instance. The correct invariant is to remove every deleted-tree category from each affected product and persist the product, never delete the product; the existing facade lookup already provides the 404 for a nonexistent id.
+
+## Proposed Remediation
+
+**Preferred**: In `CategoryServiceImpl.delete`, collect the target category and all descendants, load affected products, remove all deleted-tree categories from each product’s category set, and update each product. Never call `productService.delete` as a consequence of category deletion. Keep the category-tree deletion so descendants and their descriptions disappear. Preserve the existing facade lookup so a missing category throws `ResourceNotFoundException` and is rendered as HTTP 404.
+
+Add focused core tests covering a product assigned only to the deleted tree, a product also assigned to a surviving category, and a parent with descendants. Assert products and their fields remain while memberships are removed, and add API coverage for deleted/missing category 404 and empty category product listings.
+
+**Alternatives** (optional):
+- Delete join-table rows with a repository-level bulk operation before deleting categories, then avoid loading products; this may be more efficient but is less safe for entity-cache consistency and does not itself prove product retention.
+
+**Files likely to change**:
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/services/catalog/category/CategoryServiceImpl.java`
+- `sm-core/src/test/java/com/salesmanager/test/catalog/CategoryTest.java`
+- `sm-shop/src/test/java/com/salesmanager/test/shop/integration/category/CategoryManagementAPIIntegrationTest.java`
+
+**Tests to add or update**:
+- Service-level regression test proving category-only products survive with stock/price data and surviving-category products retain that category.
+- Descendant deletion regression test proving all descendant memberships are removed without deleting products.
+- API tests proving deleted and nonexistent categories return 404 and category product listing is empty.
+
+## Risks & Considerations
+
+- Product updates must occur in a transaction and use the managed category collection so join-table rows are removed before category deletion.
+- Existing carts/orders must not be modified; preserving product rows and their identifiers is essential.
+- Large categories may require batching in the future; the minimal fix retains current loading behavior.
+- Category deletion remains destructive for category records and descendants, as required.
+
+## Open Questions
+
+- [NEEDS CLARIFICATION: The integration test fixture/database startup command is not documented in the ticket; validate the endpoint behavior with the repository’s available Maven test profile.]
